\begin{example}%[Unsatisfiable, Fig.~\ref{fig:ex-unsat}]
Consider the formula $\mathrm{AG}_{[0,4]}\tEU{p}{q}{[1,2]} \land \mathrm{AG}_{[0,3]}\neg q$.

At $N_0$ both outer modalities are releases; their in-window unfolding asserts the released formulas
$\tEU{p}{q}{[1,2]}$ and $\neg q$ and carries $\AX\mathrm{AG}_{[0,4]}\tEU{p}{q}{[1,2]}^\bullet$ as well as $\AX\mathrm{AG}_{[0,3]}\neg q^\bullet$ in the context, giving $N_1$ on the
window $x\in[0,3]$ (the tighter, from $\mathrm{AG}_{[0,3]}\neg q$). The inner cover of $\tEU{p}{q}{[1,2]}$ now computes $\Sat(\tEU{p}{q}{[1,2]})$ by
the unfolding \ruleref{Uunf}. Its {stop} branch demands $q$ at $x\in[1,2]$, but the context keeps
$\neg q$ throughout $[0,3]\supseteq[1,2]$, so $\{q,\neg q\}$ closes; its {go} branch defers the
eventuality and elapses time, and once $x>2$ the window has passed with $q$ never permitted, so the
eventuality is permanently pending and closes. Both branches close, so $\Sat(\tEU{p}{q}{[1,2]})=\emptyset$ at this
onset and the universality test $Z\subseteq\Sat(\tEU{p}{q}{[1,2]})$ fails: $N_0$ is closed and the formula \UNSAT. 

%The onset shown is $0$; the same closure holds for every onset $s\in[0,1]$, where the
%window $[s{+}1,s{+}2]\subseteq[0,3]$ is wholly forbidden -- exactly the sub-zone the universality test
%rejects, and exactly what a single probe at, say, $s{=}4$ (window $[5,6]$, free) would miss.
\end{example}

\begin{example}%[Satisfiable, Fig.~\ref{fig:ex-sat}]
Consider only the formula $\mathrm{AG}_{[0,4]}\tEU{p}{q}{[1,2]}$. The inner unfolding's {stop} branch $\{q\}$ at $x\in[1,2]$ is now consistent, so $q$ is witnessed and $\Sat(\tEU{p}{q}{[1,2]})$ covers the onset zone; the rule \ruleref{Cov} succeeds
and discharges $\tEU{p}{q}{[1,2]}$. The outer release re-imposes $\mathrm{AG}_{[0,4]}\tEU{p}{q}{[1,2]}$
across its window $[0,4]$ and then \emph{lapses} through the after-case: past $x=4$ the obligation is gone and
the node is free, where a stutter loop is time-progressing -- $x$ is extrapolated above $M=4$ -- hence
accepting. A bounded $\mathrm{AG}$ does not loop \emph{inside} its window, where the clock is capped at the
bound; its accepting cycle lives in the lapsed tail. The marking is successful and $\mathrm{AG}_{[0,4]}\tEU{p}{q}{[1,2]}$ is
\SAT; a one-location witness with $p$ everywhere and $q$ reachable within $[1,2]$ of each
onset realises it.
\end{example}
